  "title": "Counting under branched twists is #P-hard",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "Counting the colourings of cubic bipartite Pfaffian graphs under signings is #P-hard (Turing), with oracle instances whose junction squares all have three twisted branches, while on such graphs the knot's own sign class (SEYMOUR-COUNT B) and every unbranched twist (TWIST-COUNT A) count in polynomial time: a two-sided boundary. Source (Theorem 3, known: Fan-Cai 2020, Holant([0,1,1,0] | =3) #P-hard), replayed independently: from #Positive-NAE-3SAT (Creignou-Hermann 1996) the copies of a variable lie on a cycle of merge paths of k capped lines acting by (3M)^k = 9^k (P+ + (-1/3)^k P-), last lines closed by absorbers through dead caps; #col(G_k) = 2^(D/3) 9^(kD) p((-1/3)^k) with p(1) = #formula, recovered from D + 1 values of k. Theorem 4 composes this with SOFT-CROSS A's universality. Checked on six formulas (0 to 6 solutions, graphs up to 2710 vertices), p computed independently.",
